<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- When creating a new activity, make a copy of this file with appropriate name -->
<worksheet xml:id="arithmetic-real">
  <title>Real Number Arithmetic</title>

  <!-- Optional introduction -->
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Let <m>A</m> be the set of all Cauchy sequences of rational numbers.  Let <m>\mathbb{R}</m> be the set of equivalence classes of <m>A</m> under the equivalence relation we defined previously.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m> (so <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> are equivalence classes whose elements are Cauchy sequences of rational numbers).  How would you define <m>X+Y</m>?  Describe it as a process, and make sure that your answer is in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> as defined above.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>X,Y \in \mathbb{R}</m>.  How would you define <m>X \cdot Y</m>?  Describe it as a process, and make sure that your answer is in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> as defined above.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>\{a_n\}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> are Cauchy sequences such that <m>[\{ a_n \}] = [\{2,2,2,2, \ldots \}]</m> and <m>[\{ b_n \}] = [\{ 3,3,3,3, \ldots \}]</m>.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            What is your guess for <m>[\{2,2,2,2, \ldots \}] \cdot [\{ 3,3,3,3, \ldots \}]</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Suppose you use the sequences <m>\{ a_n \}</m> and <m>\{ b_n \}</m> as representatives of <m>[\{2,2,2,2, \ldots \}]</m> and <m>[\{ 3,3,3,3, \ldots \}]</m>.  Prove that <m>[\{ a_n \}] \cdot [\{ b_n \}]</m> is the same as your guess in part (a).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Explain how the addition and multiplication defined in #1 and #2 could potentially be ambiguous.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (Not on quiz) Explain what you must assume and what you must prove in order for addition in <m>\mathbb{R}</m> to be well defined.  Then try proving that addition is well defined.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        (Not on quiz) Prove as many of the field axioms as you can for <m>\mathbb{R}</m> with addition and multiplication defined above.  You may use the fact that <m>\mathbb{Q}</m> is a field.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>